---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const path = '/daily-tarot-guide';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How to Read Tarot Daily — A Complete Beginner's Guide"
  description="A practical guide to reading tarot every day: how to set a routine, ask good questions, and keep a daily journal."
  path={path}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to Read Tarot Daily</h1>
    <p>
      A daily tarot practice isn't about predicting the future. It's about building a relationship with your
      own intuition, one card at a time.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Set a time and a place</h2>
    <p>
      Same chair, same time of day, same ritual. The repetition trains your subconscious to take the reading
      seriously. Morning works for most people — the day is ahead of you and the cards frame it.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Shuffle with intention</h2>
    <p>
      Don't shuffle to randomize. Shuffle to focus. While you shuffle, hold the question in your mind. When
      the deck feels "ready," stop.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Ask a clear question</h2>
    <p>
      Open-ended questions beat yes/no questions. <em>"What do I need to know about my work this week?"</em>
      gives the cards room to speak. <em>"Will I get the job?"</em> closes the door.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Read position by position</h2>
    <p>
      In a nine-card spread, each position has a theme. Don't read the cards as a list — read them as a
      story that moves from The Present to The Outcome.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Journal every reading</h2>
    <p>
      Write down what you drew, what you thought it meant, and what actually happened. After 30 days you'll
      see patterns you didn't expect.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Reject gently, accept easily</h2>
    <p>
      If a reading doesn't resonate, set it aside and try once more. If a second reading also misses, accept
      that the first one was the message. Today's cards are today's cards.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a class="btn" href="/">Start your daily reading →</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
